A1. Quick! Get a reporter. This might lead up to a tiptop story (two words). ~ FIREESCAPE
A9. Knotty problems. ~ BOWS
A13. What the old hens did to bring down the price of eggs. ~ LAID
A14. The last time we saw this one she was browsing in "Green Pastures." (Plur.) ~ COWS
A15. The kind of feet an elephant has. ~ CUBIC
A16. This is all past and gone. ~ AGO
A17. This kind of a fellow makes a good comrade. ~ HAIL
A18. What Mayor Walker may have to face. ~ CAMERA
A19. This might bear repeating, but even then it will be just tolerable. ~ SO
A20. A little mischief. ~ ELF
A21. If it hadn't been for this the soldiers would never have received their bonus. ~ WAR
A22. Don't give it up, you may get it still. ~ YET
A23. The way some of our great statesmen started out in life. ~ BAREFOOT
A26. Just one of the boys. ~ NED
A27. A preferred share. ~ LIONS
A29. What most of the ads advise one to do. ~ USE
A30. A good thing to give a drowning man. ~ AID
A32. A good way to spend a vacation. ~ IDLY
A33. It takes skill for this. ~ ART
A34. What naughty boys who smoke become. ~ ILL
A35. In other words, "I pass." ~ BY
A36. What the city traffic may do to the farmer's goat. ~ GET
A37. "Cut it out." ~ OPERATE
A39. Whose fault was it that the dinner was delayed ? ~ HIS
A40. If this doesn't sound funny, repeat it until it does. ~ HA
A41. If you don't seem to get the connection here, try it nevertheless. ~ BUT
A42. A group of Chicagoans who are very democratic. ~ INS
A43. What the warblers do. ~ LILT
A44. It is customary to always do this before putting over any deal. ~ CUT
A45. What one must do to get across on Broadway. ~ ACT
A46. This might lead up to matrimony. ~ AISLE
A47. The kind of a baby Chas. A. Lindbergh Jr. is. ~ BOY
A48. Instead of getting it in the neck this fellow got his in the heel. ~ ACHILLES
A50. All there is between a chicken and a king. ~ ALA
A51. What many people will soon do to the mountains and seashore. ~ HIE
A52. What a plunger takes when he plunges. ~ DIP
A53. Everything here from A to Z. ~ PI
A54. This one makes a business of scraping up acquaintances. ~ BARBER
A56. If there are 2000 pounds in a ton, how many of these are in a pound? ~ DOGS
A57. Don't let a little thing like this frighten you. ~ BOO
A58. A dry subject. ~ ESSAY
A59. In the company of. ~ WITH
A60. This card always takes the "jack." ~ MENU
A61. Keep this and you will never lose your happy home. ~ DEED
A62. These were once cast for the stage, but as yet they have never been thrown on the screen. ~ VEGETABLES

D1. Pack this in your old kit bag and it may lighten up your troubles. ~ FLASHLIGHT
D2. A villainous character. ~ IAGO
D3. For kings may come and kings may go, but this goes on forever in Spain. ~ RIO
D4. This shows it has a past. ~ ED
D5. This one receives so many tips that it always has a good balance. ~ SCALE
D6. Where is that reporter? This should also make a good headliner. ~ COIFFURE
D7. The point of this is rather boring. ~ AWL
D8. If you find this one is after "your loving wife," don't take it too seriously. ~ PS
D9. Just a vagabond. ~ BUM
D10. This is one thing the young moderns have never done. ~ OBEYED
D11. What many students have done to raise dough. ~ WIRED
D12. "Get thee hence, Maria." ~ SCAT
D15. This one once ran for the president of the United States, but now is old and forgotten. ~ CAR
D17. Whose fault was it that the biscuits were burned? ~ HERS
D18. Mouse trap. ~ CAT
D21. This country has had an overproduction of this, but don't blame it on the administration. ~ WOE
D23. What Mr. Suburbanite must do to his breakfast in order that he may also do it to the 8:30 train. ~ BOLT
D24. An uncertain Scotch quantity. ~ ANY
D25. The kind of eggs they get in South Africa. ~ OSTRICH
D26. We are giving you something for nothing here. ~ NIL
D28. This is something we have been hunting for. ~ IDEA
D30. The spirit of England and the ginger of America. ~ ALE
D31. The kind of sounds that sometimes come from the cellar. Spirits! Explosions! Gurgles! ~ MYSTERIOUS
D33. This is suitable for this space. ~ APT
D34. We have reached our wits' end for an explanation for this. ~ ITS
D35. Before April 7 this was the biggest thing in Chicago,-but now it is only a memory. ~ BILL
D37. Nobody home. ~ OUT
D38. What a good humorous puzzle is to that grouch poison that comes at the end of a heck of a day. ~ ANTIDOTE
D39. This sounds like a snake in the grass. ~ HISS
D41. What a mob of women do around a bargain counter. ~ BUY
D43. What an upright person does not do. ~ LIE
D44. This kind of a joke is very common. ~ COARSE
D45. This can make even a dummy look attractive. ~ ACE
D46. Beautiful but cold and distant. ~ ALPS
D47. No more thrills. ~ BLASE
D48. Everyone takes this and it has a very great circulation. ~ AIR
D49. This is the way Dora was headed when we saw her last. ~ LIGHT
D50. Where the world is waiting for the sunrise. ~ ABED
D51. This makes the best kind of a day. ~ HEY
D53. They dunk this down in Tennessee. ~ PONE
D55. What those Joliet prisoners are in now. ~ BAD
D56. A good thing to do for a quick turnover in real estate. ~ DIG
D57. A Babylonian deity. ~ BEL
D59. Here is another one of those partnership words. ~ WE
D60. This might stand for muffed balls but some players never do. ~ MB


